{
  "platform_id": "7CiUfsviMGI",
  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:7CiUfsviMGI",
    "purpose": "Explains the history of the New Balance 990 running sneaker, from its 1982 launch to the 990 V6, and why a shoe engineered for running became a lifestyle and 'dad' shoe. Michael tests the 990 V6 on a five-mile run and compares it with a modern Brooks running shoe.",
    "video_type": "explainer",
    "thesis": "New Balance's orthopedic roots, Boston customer base and stubborn refusal to drop its gray, leather, made-in-USA formula made the 990 V1 a groundbreaking running shoe. The same refusal let modern lightweight running shoes pass it by, so it became a stable, comfortable lifestyle shoe.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "New Balance began as an orthopedic insole maker; founder William Riley drew on the three points of balance in a chicken's foot.",
      "Being founded in Boston, with its medical, tech and university crowd, shaped a nerdy, wealthy, health-focused customer base.",
      "The 1982 990 V1 cost $100, the most expensive running shoe of its time, and had a thermoplastic heel counter, endcap technology (EVA foam inside a polyurethane rim) and premium mesh and suede.",
      "Gray became New Balance's signature as a deliberately unflashy, rebellious choice.",
      "Competitors won youth culture through athlete endorsements, while New Balance marketed itself as 'endorsed by no one'.",
      "Across six versions, the 990 kept its leather, weight and stiffness, so it faded from serious running.",
      "The 990 V6 uses less leather, a cage structure, more mesh, a segmented outsole and updated foams, and is still made in Massachusetts.",
      "In the run test the 990 V6 felt heavy, stiff and clunky next to a light, soft Brooks running shoe.",
      "Verdict: recommended as a lifestyle or walking shoe, not as a modern running shoe."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "The legendary 1982 release",
        "summary": "Cold open on the $100 990 V1 of 1982, its gray color, and the question of why nobody runs in it today. Michael sets out the agenda and plans a five-mile test run.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Origins and chicken feet",
        "summary": "New Balance's roots in orthopedics and arch support, set alongside other foot-comfort pioneers. Covers William Riley's chicken-foot inspiration and how a Boston founding shaped its customers.",
        "first_segment": 3,
        "last_segment": 9
      },
      {
        "title": "Sponsored segment",
        "summary": "Caldera + Lab skincare sponsor read, followed by a run check-in at mile one and a half.",
        "first_segment": 9,
        "last_segment": 12
      },
      {
        "title": "Technical innovations",
        "summary": "The 1982 competition and what the 990 V1 brought, demonstrated on a 574: thermoplastic heel counter, endcap EVA/polyurethane midsole, uncompromising materials and the gray colorway.",
        "first_segment": 12,
        "last_segment": 16
      },
      {
        "title": "Competition and decline",
        "summary": "Nike and others won youth culture through athlete endorsements while New Balance went 'endorsed by no one'. The 990 V1 to V6 drifted out of the running world. Includes a run check-in at mile 3.3.",
        "first_segment": 16,
        "last_segment": 19
      },
      {
        "title": "Evolution of the 990",
        "summary": "What 43 years of updates changed in the 990 V6: less leather, a cage structure, more mesh, a segmented outsole and updated endcap foams, with US manufacturing and material quality kept.",
        "first_segment": 20,
        "last_segment": 22
      },
      {
        "title": "From running to lifestyle",
        "summary": "Why gray and stubbornness define the brand, with the Steve Jobs and Apple connection. A side-by-side with a light knit Brooks running shoe shows the 990's weight, and the shoe's adoption by dads is explained.",
        "first_segment": 23,
        "last_segment": 28
      },
      {
        "title": "Final verdict on the 990",
        "summary": "After the five-mile run, Michael decides the 990 V6 won't be his running shoe and recommends it for lifestyle and walking use instead.",
        "first_segment": 28,
        "last_segment": 29
      }
    ],
    "passages": [
      {
        "role": "intro",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "starts_with": "Picture this. It's the 1980s and",
        "last_segment": 3,
        "ends_with": "What happened along the way that stopped that?",
        "note": "Cold open on the 1982 990 V1, greeting at George E. Martin Memorial Field, and the video's agenda."
      },
      {
        "role": "aside",
        "first_segment": 3,
        "starts_with": "I'm also going to go for a five mile run",
        "last_segment": 3,
        "ends_with": "So, maybe this will help.",
        "note": "Plans a five-mile run in the 990s and mentions his poor running form."
      },
      {
        "role": "sponsor",
        "first_segment": 9,
        "starts_with": "Of course, I'm pretty terrified of aging",
        "last_segment": 12,
        "ends_with": "I do recommend them. Thanks again, Caldera.",
        "note": "Sponsor read for Caldera + Lab men's skincare, led in by a childhood story about fearing aging."
      },
      {
        "role": "aside",
        "first_segment": 16,
        "starts_with": "I guess kind of, you know, it's like Rob Stark",
        "last_segment": 16,
